 {
  "id": "S218",
  "article_id": "gnews:068a24e2743bab750f43f048",
  "title": "Nvidia vs. Strategy: Which Stock Has a Better Chance of Surging 10x by 2036?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) needs no introduction. It's at the center of the artificial intelligence (AI) boom, supplying industry-leading hardware and software for the infrastructure build-out.\nInvestors might have heard of Strategy ( MSTR -3.06% ) . It's a $32 billion enterprise software business that morphed into a Bitcoin treasury company.\nBetween these two stocks, whose recent performances are on opposite ends of the spectrum, which has a better chance of surging 10x by 2036?\nNvidia's incredible",
  "passage": "…s allowing the business to repurchase its common stock and preferred shares and sell its Bitcoin stack when advantageous.\nStrategy is a financial engineering machine. It funnels constantly debasing fiat currency into Bitcoin, a finite asset. The former depreciates perpetually, while the latter has appreciated tremendously in the past. Investors must believe that Strategy can take advantage of these contrasts to capture more value over time.\nIf you're not bullish on Bitcoin, then you'll lean toward Nvidia. But the AI powerhouse isn't without its own set of risks. Uncertainty stems from a possible slowdown in AI spending, which could happen if enterprises don't see the returns they hoped for. Moreover, Nvidia's major customers are developing in-house chips to become self-reliant.\nThere's also risk and uncertainty regarding Bitcoin's long-term adoption, which isn't guaranteed, although I believe it has a bright future . And Strategy must operate with heightened focus on risk management.\nLooking forward to a decade from now, I think Strategy has higher upside. This is supported by the view that it's a leveraged position tied to Bitcoin. Should the cryptocurrency's price rise 10-fold by 2036, a significantly smaller gain than what it achieved in the past 10 years, then Strategy should perform even better.\nBy Neil Patel – Jul 4, 2026 at 12:07PM EST\nNvidia's winning stock returns are a direct result of the insatiable demand for AI hardware and software, which has lifted …",
  "statement": "Moreover, Nvidia's major customers are developing in-house chips to become self-reliant."
 },
 {
  "id": "S219",
  "article_id": "gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e",
  "title": "Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "Micron Technology ( MU +4.26% ) has had a phenomenal 2026 so far, rising around 240%, though it was up around 300% until a few days ago before artificial intelligence (AI)-centric stocks started to sell off. Regardless, Micron has had a great run, and it isn't looking to slow down anytime soon.\nMicron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028. If tha",
  "passage": "…emory due to the massive data center build-out, but supply hasn't kept up with this unprecedented demand. As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\nAs mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\nNvidia has a strong read on data center build-out plans, as many clients are placing orders for devices well in advance of when they need them so they can quickly get them online once the data center infrastructure is ready. While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\nIn Micron's latest quarter, DRAM accounted for around 76% of total sales, with NAND accounting for the rest. So, for Micron to surpass Nvidia, its NAND sales would need to rise much faster than DRAM, since DRAM will likely grow at a similar rate to GPU demand.\nI don't see that happening, but that doesn't mean Micron won't be a great stock to own. Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate. Those are still impressive figures, and I won't be surprised to see Micron keep climbing the ladder toward Nvidia. However, it will likely never catch up, as Nvidi…",
  "statement": "the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion"
 },
 {
  "id": "S220",
  "article_id": "gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4",
  "title": "Startup activates nuclear microreactor live on stage to power an Nvidia RTX Spark desktop PC — firm working with Nvidia to build a 30MW closed loop AI factory that doesn’t use local water",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Startup activates nuclear microreactor live on stage to power an Nvidia RTX Spark desktop PC — firm working with Nvidia to build a 30MW closed loop AI factory that doesn’t use local water | Tom's Hardware\nStartup activates nuclear microreactor live on stage to power an Nvidia RTX Spark desktop PC — firm working with Nvidia to build a 30MW closed loop AI factory that doesn’t use local water\nBy Jowi Morales\nWill this solve the AI boom’s power and water bottlenecks?\nValar Atomics activated its Ward",
  "passage": "…d a reduction in the quality of life in the communities that surround these developments. These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in their backyard . This resistance has led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 projects in just the first quarter of 2026.\nThe public’s pushback and the resulting actions of local and state governments against power- and water-hungry data center projects are forcing both the private and public sectors to innovate. Aside from these SMRs, which will deliver the electricity demands of these data centers and other sites without affecting the national and local grid, Amazon , Microsoft , and Nvidia are also working on technologies that will cut down data center water use by up to 100%.\nThe world needs this! Even if the entire AI experiment failed, which it won't, unless Skynet gets us, propelling nuclear power to the point SMR is safe and available to the masses would be a huge win for humanity. People, communities, humanitarian organizations, etc. could all have easy access to abundant power anywhere in the world. If they could be deployed to disaster zones they could easily save thousands of lives per year provided they proved to be safe for rapid deployment.\nIt would also help reduce data center water usage by providing more power for alternative cooling methods that are less reliant on water usage. They would also pretty much solve the high electricity rates problem …",
  "statement": "Amazon , Microsoft , and Nvidia are also working on technologies that will cut down data center water use by up to 100%"
 },
 {
  "id": "S221",
  "article_id": "gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5",
  "title": "Nvidia launches revenue-sharing model for AI startups",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "The chipmaker will earn a share of cloud revenue from partners, with two data center operators named as its first participants\nNvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves.\nRevenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to Nvidia under the arrangement, as AI cloud companies provide Nvidia -powered se",
  "passage": "…tomer side, she said the model removes the need to navigate site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware bring-up before accessing large-scale compute.\nToken credits are also part of the offering, giving startups a way to access compute resources without upfront capital outlays.\nNvidia named two initial partners. Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power. The company said AI-native firms including Baseten, Fireworks AI, and Together AI represent the kind of customers the program is designed to serve.\n\"This strategic collaboration with Nvidia marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,\" said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI, in a statement.\n\"AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,\" Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies, said in a statement.\nNvidia framed the program as a response to a structural shift in AI demand, from model development toward production inference workloads that require continuously operating infrastructure at scale. The company said emerging AI companies have historically had limited access to capital-intensive compute resources, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing.\nThe move comes as Nvidia contends with growing competition from c…",
  "statement": "\"This strategic collaboration with Nvidia marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,\""
 },
 {
  "id": "S222",
  "article_id": "gnews:bdcb0ed0921c2e9ae241083c",
  "title": "Palantir’s NVIDIA AI Collab Could Be a Game-Changer That Marks a Turn in the Stock",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "Palantir shares surged 18% in a week as investors rotate from semis into AI software.\nA new deal using Nvidia's Nemotron to build government AI models squeezes short sellers and may justify Palantir's 145x trailing P/E multiple.\nDespite Palantir's 85% revenue growth quarter, Nvidia may offer better value.\nShares of Palantir ( NASDAQ:PLTR ) have been bouncing back, now up close to 18% in a week, and it might be a good idea to step out of the way if you're short or are hanging onto bearish put opt",
  "passage": "…ting shadows on the AI revolution in its tracks.\nEither way, the company's latest partnership with Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) brings together two of the biggest winners from the AI boom. And while both names have fallen a bit out of favor in recent months, I still think that the expanded partnership stands out as a huge win-win, especially as the AI leaders look to guide the world into the next phase of AI. For Dr. Burry, though, it's two of his shorts working together in a way that may or may not cause the man to cover more of his bearish positions.\nThe Palantir-NVIDIA collab has been a driving force for Palantir shares\nAs Palantir and Nvidia go after the opportunity to be had in sovereign AI. With Palantir now inking a deal to use Nvidia's Nemotron to build models for the government, it feels like the shorts, like Dr. Burry, have one extra thing to be worried about, as they feel just a bit of a squeeze. Indeed, when it comes to an oversold stock, sudden announcements like this can really cause a bit of anxiety on the part of those who've bet against a stock that's been steadily sinking.\nAs the king of ontology joins with the king of AI compute, perhaps there's every reason for the bulls to justify Palantir's 145 times trailing price-to-earnings (P/E) multiple. After all, investors seemed to ignore that blowout quarter, which could be just the start, as the firm brings more clients aboard while extending collabs with the likes of Nvidia.\nUntil Dr. Burry covers the rest …",
  "statement": "With Palantir now inking a deal to use Nvidia's Nemotron to build models for the government"
 },
 {
  "id": "S223",
  "article_id": "gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce",
  "title": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\nJAKARTA - Chinese artificial intelligence (AI) startup DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei, which have so far been used in the development of the company's AI models.\nAccording to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\nUnlike chips use",
  "passage": "…posting job openings on general recruitment platforms.\nDespite becoming one of the symbols of China's AI resurgence, DeepSeek has maintained a low profile and has not responded to Reuters' request for confirmation regarding the project.\nGlobally, DeepSeek's move follows a broader trend among major AI companies developing their own chips. OpenAI introduced its first inference chip, called Jalapeno, last month in collaboration with Broadcom, while Anthropic is also reportedly considering developing its own AI chips.\nFor DeepSeek, chip development carries greater strategic importance because US export restrictions have prevented Chinese companies from accessing Nvidia's most advanced AI chips.\nThe Chinese government has also continued to encourage domestic technology companies to build their own semiconductor supply chains.\nDeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng acknowledged in a 2024 interview that chip export restrictions posed a challenge for the company.\nPreviously, DeepSeek used a combination of Nvidia and Huawei chips. The base model supporting DeepSeek R1 was trained using Nvidia's H800 chips, a product specifically designed for the Chinese market before the US government banned its export in late 2023.\nIn recent months, the company has increasingly relied on Huawei's ecosystem. In April, DeepSeek launched its V4 model, which had been optimised for Huawei Ascend chips, while Huawei revealed that training for the V4-Flash model also used Ascend processors.\nThe launch also drove a surge in demand for Huawei's Asc…",
  "statement": "The Chinese government has also continued to encourage domestic technology companies to build their own semiconductor supply chains."
 },
 {
  "id": "S224",
