 S164 | By Ben Wodecki
AMS-Osram, an Austrian semiconductor manufacturer of light and sensor solutions, poached a networking director from Nvidia.
Ashkan Seyedi had served as Nvidia’s lead for technical product marketing on its co-packaged and pluggable interconnect solutions since 2021. In a LinkedIn post , Sayedi revealed he’s taking on the role of VP an | …illion .
MicroLEDs have become increasingly seen as an attractive alternative technology to power data center interconnects. Traditional copper links have a limited reach of around two meters, while optical links such as fiber optics can reach longer distances, but at the expense of high power consumption and lower reliability.
Microsoft is among those exploring potential microLED links, with its Mosaic prototype interconnect system capable of maintaining high network speeds with further reach while using far less power.
Those efforts were initially detailed in a paper published last September, but have since matured via a collaboration with Taiwanese fabless semiconductor company MediaTek. The pair plans to launch microLED-based data center networking interconnects for partners by late 2027 .
AMS-Osram’s efforts, meanwhile, will see work toward developing microLED optical interconnects for inside the rack as well as chip-to-chip communication between graphic processing units (GPUs) and high-bandwidth memory, CEO Aldo Kamper told investors during its Q1 2026 earnings call back in May.
“I’m looking forward to meeting everyone and contributing to the exciting journey ahead,” Seyedi said. “Together we'll define and create the next front of optical interconnect solutions. More to come.”
Prior to joining Nvidia, Sayedi was a senior research scientist at Hewlett Packard Enterprise (HPE), where he worked on a commercial process design kit (PDK) for silicon photonics.

 S165 | AI Innovators Adopt NVIDIA Vera — Why Max Single-Threaded CPU at Scale Matters | NVIDIA Blog
AI Innovators Adopt NVIDIA Vera — Why Max Single-Threaded CPU at Scale Matters
NVIDIA Vera exemplifies a new class of CPU, architected for the era of agents and being adopted by AI innovators including Perplexity; NVIDIA CPU roadmap continues with the NVIDI | …ads faster. Partners have measured 3x faster large-scale SQL analytics with Starburst and up to 6x lower latency on real-time streaming with Redpanda, both against leading x86 server CPUs.
Agent work isn’t one workload. An agent runs tools and sandboxes, processes data, serves requests and trains the next model with reinforcement learning — and all of it leans on the same strengths.
One Vera handles the whole range, rather than requiring a different CPU for each kind of work. And because Vera is the same CPU that hosts the GPUs in NVIDIA Vera Rubin and powers the NVIDIA BlueField-4 STX storage processor, the whole AI factory runs on one architecture and one toolchain.
And NVIDIA’s not done. NVIDIA’s next-generation Rosa CPU with the Rigel core will continue the company’s CPU roadmap for the agentic AI era. Rigel is NVIDIA’s next-generation Arm v9.2 CPU core, delivering higher per-core performance than Olympus while keeping the same silicon footprint. Key improvements include better instruction delivery, a larger L2 cache and more efficient memory handling.
In the agentic AI era, there will be billions of agents, and every one of them will turn to a CPU to act, check, retrieve, execute and verify. In this new market, completed agent work is the product. Faster agent loops help every GPU spend more time generating revenue producing work and less time waiting.
NVIDIA Vera is the CPU built for that future.

