S185 position=5559 earlier: he industry has examined these challenges through a “compute-constrained” lens, treating compute as a scarce resource, forcing compromises to fit complex algorithms within the strict limitations of CPU power and performance budgets. NVIDIA AI Aerial is changing that paradigm. With parallel computing, compute is no longer the bottleneck for operating within existing power budgets. By embracing an AI-native, highly parallelized architecture, we don’t have to ask, “How can I squeeze more into the same compute?” Instead, we ask, “How can we reinvent the algorithm across the full stack to maximize the spectrum?” This “algorithms-first” approach enables the network to finally run the complex tracking and pairing models required to close the massive MIMO performance gap. This blog describes the benefits of GPU acceleration in the RAN, and how AI-native RAN helps close the massive MIMO performance gap. It shows how NVIDIA AI Aerial enables a new class of Layer 1 and Layer 2 algorithms designed to unlock greater spectral efficiency in real-world deployments. Why GPU acceleration benefits spectral efficiency Modern RAN pipelines consist of mathematically dense algorithmic tasks, with highly specific compute characteristics. A scheduler that evaluates a wider combinatorial space, a channel estimator that learns from richer observations, or a beamformer that adapts jointly across users can improve throughput and quality of service for user applications. Table 1 is a breakdown of the highest-impact RAN workloads, their compute characteristics, and why GPU acceleration is required to execute them effectively: The following sections review two workloads—beamforming and link adaptation—and explore how GPUs unlock new spectral efficiency gains. Beamforming quality determines how effectively a network converts channel knowledge into usable throughput. Classical methods are effective but are shaped by computational compromise, relying on simplified models to meet limited budgets. As systems move to higher antenna counts and denser MU-MIMO operation, these simplifications result in lost signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio (SINR) and lower realized throughput. ML-based beamforming weight generation solves this by using richer channel information for weights, though it raises the compute burden sharply. NVIDIA analysis shows that in a 64T64R MU-MIMO scenario with 16 users and 2 layers per user, where users are randomly assigned SNRs in the [0-20] dB range, AI beamforming requires significantly more FLOPs than traditional regularized Zero Forcing (rZF), but this increase in compute produces a spectral efficiency gain of up to 1.62x higher throughput at 32 layers compared to the traditional zero-forcing beamforming algorithm. Even at a lower number of layers, ML-beamforming delivers 1.28x throughput improvement. GPU compute enables higher-quality beam weight generation at the scale and latency required for RAN deployment. This connects directly to field validation. 

S193 position=2004 earlier: Price change for Nvidia stock in last 1 year: 24% $NVDA Stock Price as of Jul. 9: $203 52-Week High: $236 $NVDA Stock Price Target: $302 TD Cowen reiterated its Buy rating on Nvidia ( NVDA ) stock and named it a Top Pick, maintaining a $275 price target implying roughly 35% upside from where shares closed. The call came after the firm hosted an investor lunch with Nvidia’s leadership team, including CEO Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, and Head of Investor Relations Toshiya Hari. Analyst Joshua Buchalter said Nvidia’s message to investors is clear: computing power remains in short supply, and the market is thinking about Nvidia’s technology far too narrowly. He pointed out that Nvidia’s hardware and software work together, or “co-design,” in a way that gives the company a real edge as AI keeps evolving from general chatbot-style applications toward more specialized, industry-specific tools. To put some numbers behind that, Nvidia estimates about 20% of its business comes from supporting frontier AI models built by companies like OpenAI and Anthropic. Meanwhile, TD Cowen estimates that Nvidia’s revenue from enterprise applications across multiple industries is in the low-to-mid-teens range. That’s a meaningful chunk of business coming from outside the flashy AI chatbot headlines, which is part of Buchalter’s point about Nvidia’s opportunity being underappreciated. TD Cowen also highlighted evidence that computing capacity is genuinely tight right now. Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices. All of that points to demand outpacing supply. Nvidia’s own recent quarterly results back up the strength story. The company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, marking its third straight quarter of accelerating growth. Data center revenue alone hit $75 billion, up 92% year over year. Nvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: 

S196 position=6408 earlier: ed that emerging AI companies have historically faced significant constraints in accessing capital-intensive infrastructure—even long-term contractual commitments have often been insufficient to secure financing for compute capacity procurement. This means that numerous AI-native firms, model developers, and inference service providers face lengthy delays when scaling their computing capabilities: site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware commissioning—each step can take several months or even longer. The promise of this new model is to realign economic structures so these groups can rapidly access full-stack accelerated computing capabilities without waiting for the traditional infrastructure build-out cycle to complete. Underlying Rationale: Addressing the Core Challenge of GPU Financing Reportedly, GPUs typically represent the most expensive component in AI data centers. For chip buyers with lower credit ratings, securing sufficient loans is itself a major hurdle. A data center executive commented on such NVIDIA deals as “killing two birds with one stone.” He explained that if NVIDIA merely provided a guarantee for a data center facility lease, “you’d still face the question of ‘how to finance the GPUs’”; but if NVIDIA commits to purchasing any unsold compute capacity within the facility, “the GPU financing issue is resolved—and so is the data center’s financing problem.” In other words, NVIDIA’s backstop commitment effectively serves as a credit enhancement tool, enabling emerging cloud service providers—who would otherwise struggle to obtain bank loans—to mobilize significantly larger amounts of capital and accelerate data center construction. Strategic Intent: Breaking the Dominance of Large Customers NVIDIA’s launch of these initiatives is grounded in a clear strategic context. Currently, a small number of large cloud service providers—including Amazon, Microsoft, SpaceX, Oracle, Meta, and Google—purchase the majority of NVIDIA’s chip output. However, several of these companies are developing their own competitive AI chips, posing a potential threat to NVIDIA. To reduce its reliance on these giant clients, NVIDIA has spent the past few years actively supporting a cohort of emerging GPU cloud service providers, such as CoreWeave. The newly announced 'AI Compute Partnership Program' represents a continuation and deepening of this strategy. According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion. Capital Commitments: From Equity Investments to Capacity Guarantees NVIDIA’s capital commitments in this direction have already become substantial. To date, NVIDIA has invested several billion dollars in equity stakes in multiple emerging cloud service providers and, in certain cases, 

S197 position=2841 earlier: How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally NVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.… By Danielle Liverance NVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade. AMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewarding investors who bet on chips with room to reprice rather than perfection already baked in. Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in. NVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else. That is the paradox: the company at the center of the AI buildout has become the black sheep of the chip rally. Advanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has ripped 171.25% year to date. Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ) is up a stunning 304.62%. Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment. Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns. But the broader story is one of semiconductor stocks rallying. The VanEck Semiconductor ETF (Nasdaq: SMH) is up 59% year-to-date. The divergence looks set to continue today. The VanEck is up 3% premarket while NVIDIA shares are up just .3%, as of 8:40 a.m. ET. NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems. The earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion. CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%. The narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. 

